WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.154 --> 00:00:05.244
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.244 --> 00:00:09.775
When I was nine years old,
I asked my parents for an allowance.

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:14.767
Since I've been diligently
feeding the cats, they agreed,

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:18.710
and we settled on $3
a week payable on Fridays.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.108
The first Friday rolls around and
I am so excited.

00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:28.330
We're sitting at the dinner table,
and my dad slowly pulls out a crisp,

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.322
$2 bill, and
then a stack of four quarters.

00:00:32.322 --> 00:00:38.098
Then he takes out these two plastic
yogurt cups with Sharpie on them.

00:00:38.098 --> 00:00:40.207
One of them says family.

00:00:40.207 --> 00:00:44.646
The other one, charity.

00:00:44.646 --> 00:00:46.651
Dad, what's with the cups?

00:00:46.651 --> 00:00:52.419
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; My parents calmly explained to me that

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:57.612
out of every paycheck come
certain contributions.

00:00:57.612 --> 00:01:02.427
And that from my $3 allowance,
$0.25 was going

00:01:02.427 --> 00:01:07.041
to a family tax and
$0.25 was going to charity.

00:01:07.041 --> 00:01:11.473
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Nine-year-old Alexis was pissed

00:01:11.473 --> 00:01:12.124
[LAUGH].

00:01:12.124 --> 00:01:16.034
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I had done all of the calculations, and

00:01:16.034 --> 00:01:19.226
it was now going to take
literally forever for

00:01:19.226 --> 00:01:22.511
me to buy my summer pass
to the amusement park.

00:01:22.511 --> 00:01:25.378
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So as you can imagine,

00:01:25.378 --> 00:01:31.082
this was my first taste of
the frustrations of personal finance.

00:01:31.082 --> 00:01:33.961
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As we all know,

00:01:33.961 --> 00:01:39.595
this really pales in comparison to what
a lot of people face on this front.

00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:44.651
People rank money-related
concerns as worse than death and

00:01:44.651 --> 00:01:48.825
just behind being alone for
the rest of your life.

00:01:48.825 --> 00:01:53.006
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And maybe it's no wonder,

00:01:53.006 --> 00:01:57.268
when we look at it and
we find out that almost half of

00:01:57.268 --> 00:02:02.151
Americans don't have $400
in case of an emergency.

00:02:02.151 --> 00:02:08.175
And some people really
can't make this math work.

00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:11.795
But I know a lot of highly educated,

00:02:11.795 --> 00:02:16.868
highly motivated people
with resources who still

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:22.434
seem to make irrational
decisions about their money.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:27.851
I spent the past seven years working with
people who know a lot about how you and

00:02:27.851 --> 00:02:29.526
I manage our finances.

00:02:29.526 --> 00:02:35.154
I was at the Consumer Financial Protection
Bureau in DC doing strategy consulting for

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:39.000
big retail banks and
most recently at a consumer FinTech.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:41.341
And even in these environments,

00:02:41.341 --> 00:02:45.112
I came across people who
made irrational decisions.

00:02:45.112 --> 00:02:49.490
One day, I asked my teammate,
what about his money habits?

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:55.982
He's an expert in helping banks manage
risk and plan for the long term.

00:02:55.982 --> 00:03:03.193
He let me know all his savings in
a checking account earning zero interest.

00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:07.777
There has got to be something
deeper going on here.

00:03:07.777 --> 00:03:12.726
The solution, I argue, is to start from

00:03:12.726 --> 00:03:17.541
psychology, not from spreadsheets.

00:03:17.541 --> 00:03:22.636
Picking a psychology first
approach to personal finance

00:03:22.636 --> 00:03:28.683
gives me hope that we can each learn
to better manage our own money and

00:03:28.683 --> 00:03:34.858
also change the way that we as
a society talk about personal finance.

00:03:34.858 --> 00:03:36.370
But how can we make this happen?

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:41.507
I've come up with three steps that
I'm going to share with you today.

00:03:41.507 --> 00:03:49.716
So step one, understand your
personal financial psychology.

00:03:49.716 --> 00:03:52.620
A few examples of what
this could look like.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.715
As millennials,

00:03:53.715 --> 00:03:58.388
we're investing a lot less in the stock
market than previous generations.

00:03:58.388 --> 00:03:59.610
And experts say,

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:04.755
we may have developed risk aversion
from growing up during the recession.

00:04:04.755 --> 00:04:08.954
People from poor households
may have a scarcity mindset,

00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:13.750
which can come from having grown up
in an environment where money is

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.395
uncertain as you end up spending
more than you should be saving.

00:04:18.395 --> 00:04:22.086
Personally, I come from
a house that kept spending and

00:04:22.086 --> 00:04:27.395
personal finance top of mind, as you may
have gotten from that yogurt cup bit.

00:04:27.395 --> 00:04:30.503
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So [LAUGH] this, unfortunately,

00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:32.854
can have its own ramifications.

00:04:32.854 --> 00:04:36.410
And two weekends ago,
I was up in San Francisco and

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:41.680
I walked half a mile in 5-inch heels
instead of paying $5 for parking.

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.556
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is something I'm working on

00:04:45.556 --> 00:04:46.278
[LAUGH].

00:04:46.278 --> 00:04:48.883
So where are you coming from?

00:04:48.883 --> 00:04:53.099
And what does that lead you to do today?

00:04:53.099 --> 00:05:00.518
Step two, implement a system to
counteract those tendencies.

00:05:00.518 --> 00:05:03.999
Here at the GSB,
we talk a lot about culture building,

00:05:03.999 --> 00:05:06.812
about setting up incentives for our teams.

00:05:06.812 --> 00:05:12.174
I'm saying,
we should do the same for ourselves.

00:05:12.174 --> 00:05:16.882
And there's a lot of great new innovative
tools out there that can help with this.

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:21.401
But they only help when you
start from where you are and

00:05:21.401 --> 00:05:23.821
build a system around that.

00:05:23.821 --> 00:05:26.743
So for example, if it's risk aversion,

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:31.092
you can outsource your investing
activity to a robo advisor.

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:34.345
You can take that accountability
off of your plate and

00:05:34.345 --> 00:05:36.375
make you feel more comfortable.

00:05:36.375 --> 00:05:41.449
If the issue is a scarcity mindset,
instead, you can use an app that will help

00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:46.212
you with different saving activity,
like rounding up your purchases to

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:51.469
the nearest dollar and setting aside that
extra change into a savings account.

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:55.884
One thing we can all benefit from
is finding ways to associate our

00:05:55.884 --> 00:06:00.321
financial chores with positive
experiences and with rewards.

00:06:00.321 --> 00:06:05.233
Every month, I get a calendar
alert that says, adventure fund.

00:06:05.233 --> 00:06:08.298
And there's just a bunch of
exclamation points over here and

00:06:08.298 --> 00:06:09.569
like maybe a few emojis.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:13.006
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And this is my alert to move my extra

00:06:13.006 --> 00:06:17.938
cash from my checking account into
a high interest savings account.

00:06:17.938 --> 00:06:22.365
So that I can earn a few extra points of
interest on money that would be sitting

00:06:22.365 --> 00:06:23.344
around anyway.

00:06:23.344 --> 00:06:26.834
By finding a way to take
this financial chore and

00:06:26.834 --> 00:06:30.586
associate it with something I love,
with travel,

00:06:30.586 --> 00:06:35.995
with the feelings I'm going to have
when I actually get to use that savings,

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:40.292
I get to move that future feeling
of reward to the present.

00:06:40.292 --> 00:06:43.961
These are some of the ways that
we can actually build habits.

00:06:43.961 --> 00:06:47.099
It's the ways that we can end
up sticking to our goals.

00:06:47.099 --> 00:06:52.315
And through automation and
association with positive feelings and

00:06:52.315 --> 00:06:56.809
rewards, we can actually end
up changing our behaviors.

00:06:56.809 --> 00:06:58.881
Which leads me to step three.

00:06:58.881 --> 00:07:01.618
You have the system, now what?

00:07:01.618 --> 00:07:06.686
Share it.
A whopping 43% of Americans don't

00:07:06.686 --> 00:07:11.992
know how much money their spouse makes.

00:07:11.992 --> 00:07:12.912
Think about that.

00:07:12.912 --> 00:07:16.825
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How are we supposed to plan for

00:07:16.825 --> 00:07:20.064
shared futures when we don't
have this information?

00:07:20.064 --> 00:07:23.757
How are our loved ones supposed to
hold us accountable to our goals?

00:07:23.757 --> 00:07:29.026
How can we help the people
we care about in our lives?

00:07:29.026 --> 00:07:32.841
We're taking classes right now
on accounting and finance.

00:07:32.841 --> 00:07:37.134
We're making these huge decisions about
taking on debt, about what career we're

00:07:37.134 --> 00:07:40.711
going to have, what implication that's
going to have for our salaries.

00:07:40.711 --> 00:07:43.885
And yet,
none of us are talking about money.

00:07:47.893 --> 00:07:52.909
Even if you're in a good place with this,
I guarantee you someone

00:07:52.909 --> 00:07:58.030
close to you is sitting here with
a really bad pit in their stomach.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:03.144
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And we all have a
responsibility to change the ways that we

00:08:03.144 --> 00:08:08.563
talk about money, to take away
the shame from that conversation.

00:08:08.563 --> 00:08:13.417
So if you've recently gone
through a salary negotiation,

00:08:13.417 --> 00:08:16.530
you can help someone else get a raise.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:20.094
If you've got a great system for
your savings,

00:08:20.094 --> 00:08:23.666
you can help someone else
get over that hurdle.

00:08:23.666 --> 00:08:28.561
If you just start talking about money,

00:08:28.561 --> 00:08:35.840
you can welcome someone else
who needs help to do the same.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:41.870
Tackling these three steps understand,
your personal financial psychology,

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:46.125
implement a system to counteract
those tendencies, and

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:50.928
share it will empower each of us
to live better financial lives.

00:08:50.928 --> 00:08:57.288
And change the ways that we
as a society approach money.

00:08:57.288 --> 00:09:01.192
I care way too much about my friends and

00:09:01.192 --> 00:09:07.906
family to let them struggle
unnecessarily with their finances.

00:09:07.906 --> 00:09:11.731
You can bet someday I'm going to
tax my own kids' allowance.

00:09:11.731 --> 00:09:14.233
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And by that point,

00:09:14.233 --> 00:09:19.168
it will probably be more
like $1 instead of $0.25.

00:09:19.168 --> 00:09:21.828
So pay it forward with a little
lesson about inflation.

00:09:21.828 --> 00:09:28.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:32.557
[MUSIC]

